1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:04,180
Module 5, video 5.4, Identity and Registries.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:07,400
Use the block. Do not rebuild it. Right.

3
00:00:08,039 --> 00:00:11,140
I mean, the core principle here is that the service takes a person's identity

4
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:15,560
and, you know, an institution's record directly from the body that actually keeps them.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:16,360
Exactly.

6
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:17,900
So picture this.

7
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,220
A supplier offers to add a table of users

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:25,160
and, uh, maybe a table of institutions to your ministry's new application.

9
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,120
I mean, should you accept?

10
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:35,740
No, definitely not, because accepting this just creates local copies, meaning your data becomes, well, fragmented and siloed.

11
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:37,480
And duplicated everywhere, right?

12
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:40,060
Yeah, duplicated and super costly to change.

13
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:45,260
Basically, your application traps facts that it just has, you know, no reason to keep.

14
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:48,120
Okay, so if we avoid that,

15
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:52,940
how does the GovStack identity specification actually establish who is signing in?

16
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:59,000
Well, the specification uses OpenID Connect, and it relies on a central identity block.

17
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:04,879
The flow itself occurs in three steps. Step one, the user is sent to the identity block's own page.

18
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:08,880
Oh, so they aren't logging in on your application at all.

19
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:09,240
Right.

20
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:14,140
Then step two, the user signs in and approves exactly which facts are shared.

21
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,400
Makes sense. And the last step.

22
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:19,160
Step three, the application receives a code.

23
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:22,700
It exchanges it for a token and then reads those approved facts.

24
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:28,360
But wait, does this token exchange risk exposing a person's master identity across all these

25
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:30,320
different government applications?

26
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:37,000
You would think so, but no. The identity block gives each service a different unique identifier for the exact same person.

27
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:40,820
Oh, wow. So it's scoped just to that one specific service.

28
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:46,700
Yeah. Your application just keeps the specific identifier it was given, and absolutely no national number.

29
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:47,340
Got it.

30
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:52,340
So what does the GovStack digital registry specification require for

31
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:54,520
managing institutional records?

32
00:01:55,020 --> 00:02:04,140
Well, a register lets other systems search, read, create, and, um, update its records through open interfaces, and it authorizes which systems and users may actually do so.

33
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:10,000
So how does this operate in, uh, Progressa, the, you know, the fictional country of these examples?

34
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:15,660
Right. So say an officer in your ministry signs in through the National Identity Authority, PNIA.

35
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:23,460
When opening a case, the application reads the record for Harbourview University College directly from PHEQA's register.

36
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:26,720
Directly from the source rather than a local database.

37
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:36,760
Exactly. Now, in Progressa, this design is the ministry's own choice, not the specification's, because only PHEQA's application writes the register of institutions.

38
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:42,980
Okay, so what is the consequence if your program relies on a local copy of the college's record instead?

39
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:51,440
Well, if PHEQA updates the college's name in the register, your local copy remains duplicated and locked into one supplier.

40
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:53,680
It's just completely stuck in the past.

41
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:59,560
Yeah. Your minister could literally sign a legal decision under a name the college no longer even holds.

42
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,880
Wow, that is a massive liability.

43
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:03,700
Definitely.

44
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:10,760
The reference architecture notes that digital public infrastructure is not neutral, and it actually shapes what can be built on top of it.

45
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:13,680
Because a block paid for once serves many, right?

46
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:17,060
Right. Whereas a copy is, well, it's really just a second register.

47
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,800
So what will the demonstration show for all this?

48
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,720
The recording will show a test officer signing in through the identity sign-in.

49
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:28,100
You'll see the application showing the identifier it was given, and, uh, no national number.

50
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,740
Just that unique identifier.

51
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:35,260
Exactly. And then an institution's record is opened, read directly from PHEQA's register.

52
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,400
It will pass when the sign-in completes, no national number appears, and the record agrees with the register.

53
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,940
So what is the core instruction when designing these systems?

54
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:46,300
It comes down to this.

55
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:52,280
Take who someone is from the identity block and what an institution is from its register.

56
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:55,500
Keep the identifier you are given and no copy.

57
